  "title": "Day 5: Limits and Asymptotes (Section 2.6b) and the Sandwich Theorem",
  "body": " Day 5: Limits and Asymptotes (Section 2.6b) and the Sandwich Theorem    Last Time: We learned about one-sided and infinite limits, and how to determine when limits exist.   Today: We want to find the relationships between limits and asymptotes, and learn how to deal with a few last strange cases.     Vertical Asymptotes  We saw last time (Day 4) that when we have a vertical asymptote, we also get an limit, which takes the form after direct substitution. This relationship is even closer than that we can use limits to prove the existence of vertical asymptotes.    Definition  A line is a asymptote of the graph of a function if either     or       Problem 1  Find all vertical asymptotes of the function . Be sure to justify your answers using limits.    Step 1: Note that an infinite limit could occur when .    Step 2: We can now use limits to verify vertical asymptotes.    So we see that finding places where the of a rational function is gives us a good start on values we should check for asymptotes, but we really need to use to prove that we actually have one.      Limits at Infinity  Let's shift gears a little here: we have a decent understanding now of how to evaluate limits that end up being equal to  but what about limits at infinity? That is, how do we think about things of the form   The trick here is that just like vertical asymptotes capture what's going on when our function gets arbitrarily large (positive or negative) in the direction, asymptotes are really giving us information about what happens to as becomes arbitrarily large (positive or negative).    Definition  A line is a asymptote of the graph of a function if either     or       To find all possible horizontal asymptotes of , we compute both and .    A function has  distinct horizontal asymptotes.     Example:     Graph illustrating an exponential function approaching a horizontal asymptote as x approaches positive or negative infinity.    Again, this idea is hopefully intuitive based on a graph, but how do we discover what's going on by looking at a function algebraically? Here are 2 sets of limits that help us find horizontal asymptotes:    Problem 2  Find all horizontal asymptotes of by computing the following limits:     (a)       (b)   (exercise!)    Graph of y = 1\/x showing both branches approaching y = 0 as x approaches positive and negative infinity.   A hint for this problem: Recall the graph of .      Strategy  Let's write down generally the strategy that we used above:     Strategy: If direct substitution yields an indeterminate form of , divide term in both the numerator and denominator by the term (in absolute value) in the entire function.     Alternative: Dividing by the term (in absolute value) in the also works it simply gives a different-looking (but equivalent) expression.         Problem 3  Find all horizontal asymptotes of .   Recall that:  .     (a)  (Exercise) Consider   Hint: Let's use the alternative approach.     (b) Consider   Hint: How the exponential function behaves as approaches negative infinity.      Recap  Whenever we find asymptotes, we look for infinite limits ( asymptotes) or limits at infinity ( asymptotes).    Problem 4  Let's consider the following problem to get thinking about limits again. Find all horizontal asymptotes of the function .    Recall that:     oscillates between  and  with a period of .      ,  , , etc.      Graph of y = cos(x) oscillating periodically between y = -1 and y = 1 with period 2 pi.    First, look at (a graph might help you may use Desmos):  So there is horizontal asymptote in the negative -direction.  This shows another way a limit fails to exist: If our function oscillates getting infinitely close to a single number, the limit .      Two Interesting Phenomena of Limits     Graph of a function oscillating with unbounded amplitude so it never approaches a single number.    Oscillating without getting infinitely close to a single number  The limit DNE.      Graph of a damped oscillating function whose amplitude shrinks toward one as x approaches negative infinity.    Oscillating and infinitely close to a single number  The limit .     Now to make sure we have all horizontal asymptotes, we also check . Based on the graph, this limit appears to be , but we need a careful algebraic method to confirm it.  Here's an effective way to handle limits with bounded oscillations:    The Sandwich (or Squeeze) Theorem    Diagram illustrating the Sandwich Theorem with f(x) trapped between upper function h(x) and lower function g(x), all meeting at L.    Suppose   on some interval containing (except possibly at itself). If       ,  then we must have  also.   Note: In the theorem, can be finite or infinite.    This theorem will help us finish our problem. We'll look again at :    "
